E_PREMIUM[type];\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n```\n- Constants: 2 (`0` init, `3`, `60`) ≈ 3 → 3\n- Bindings: `countsByType`, `type`, `count`, `total` = 4\n- Invocations: `.set`, `.get`, `.get` (lookup) ≈ 3 → 6\n- Conditionals: 1 (ternary) → 4\n- Loops: 2 (`for...of` x2) → 10\n- Assignments: `total += ...`, `countsByType.set(...)` ≈ 2 → 12\n\nTotal Mass ≈ **39**\n\n**Rule Evaluation**:\n- Rule 1 (Tests Pass): all 8 green before change.\n- Rule 2 (Reveals Intent): the magic numbers `3` and `60` in the ternary hide the \"block pricing\" business rule — a reader can't tell what `3` and `60` mean without inspecting the surrounding domain knowledge.\n- Rule 3 (No Duplication): none found.\n- Rule 4 (Fewest Elements): structure is already minimal; no unnecessary abstraction.\n\n**Refactoring Applied**: Extracted magic numbers `3` and `60` into named constants `BLOCK_SIZE` and `BLOCK_PRICE`, placed alongside `ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM`.\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n```ts\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PRICE = 60;\n\nexport const computeItemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const countsByType = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const { type } of items) {\n    countsByType.set(type, (countsByType.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countsByType) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PRICE : count * ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM[type];\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n```\nMass count for the function body is unchanged structurally (same bindings/invocations/loops/conditional count — the two literals moved to module-level constants used as bindings instead of inline constants, a neutral swap under APP, ~39 → ~39).\n\nMass Change: 39 → 39 (delta 0, neutral under APP)\n\n**Reasoning**: This refactor doesn't reduce mass, but Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) outranks APP mass minimization. Naming the `3`/`60` literals as `BLOCK_SIZE`/`BLOCK_PRICE` makes the upcoming block-discount rules (4 runes, 7 runes, mixed types) self-documenting and easier to ext