re refactor):\n```ts\nexport const computeItemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const countsByType = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const { type } of items) {\n    countsByType.set(type, (countsByType.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countsByType) {\n    if (!(type in ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n    }\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM[type];\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n```\n- Constants: `0`(init), `1`, `` `Unknown item type: ${type}` `` = 3\n- Bindings: `items`, `countsByType`, `type`(loop1), `total`, `type`(loop2), `count` = 6\n- Invocations: `Map` ctor, `.set`, `.get`, `new Error`, `in` check (treated as invocation-like op) = ~4 (counting `.set`, `.get`, `new Error`, template literal not counted) → 4\n- Conditionals: `if`, ternary = 2 → mass 8\n- Loops: 2 (`for..of` x2) → mass 10\n- Assignments: `countsByType.set` (6), `total = 0` (6), `total +=` (6) = 3 → mass 18\n\nTotal Mass ≈ 3 + 6 + 8 + 8 + 10 + 18 = **53**\n\n**Refactoring Applied**: Extracted the per-type validation + pricing logic (unknown-type check and block-discount calculation) into a new helper function `computeTypePremium(type, count)`. The main loop in `computeItemsBasePremium` now simply delegates to this helper and accumulates the result.\n\nRationale — Rule 2 (Reveals Intent): The original loop mixed two concerns (validating the type exists, and computing that type's contribution to the total) inline. Separating \"compute one type's premium\" from \"aggregate premiums across types\" makes each piece single-purpose and names the per-type pricing rule explicitly (`computeTypePremium`), which will also make it easier to extend/reuse when `computeInsuranceSum` needs equivalent per-type logic.\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n```ts\nconst computeTypePremium = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (!(type in ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM)) {\n    throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  return count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM[type];\n};\n\nexport const computeItemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const countsByType = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const { type } of items) {\n    countsByType.set(type, (countsByType.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countsByType) {\n    total += computeTypePremium(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n```\n- Constants: `1`, `` `Unknown item type: ${type}` ``, `0`(init) = 3\n- Bindings: `type`, `count` (helper params), `items`, `countsByType`, `type`(loop1), `total`, `type`(loop2), `count`(loop2) = 8\n- Invocations: `.set`, `.get`, `new Error`, `computeTypePremium(...)` = 4 → mass 8\n- Conditionals: `if`, ternary = 2 → mass 8\n- Loops: 2 → mass 10\n- Assignments: `countsByType.set` (6), `total = 0` (6), `total +=` (6) = 3 → mass 18\n\nTotal Mass ≈ 3 + 8 + 8 + 8 + 10 + 18 = **55**\n\nMass Change: 53 -> 55 (delta +2, from the extra binding/invocation of introducing the helper)\n\nPer Rule 2 > APP: the small mass increase is acceptable because the extraction improves separation of concerns and sets up cleaner reuse for the upcoming `computeInsuranceSum` tests, which will likely need similar per-type premium logic without the block discount.\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Extracted `computeTypePremium(type, count)` helper from `computeItemsBasePremium`'s loop body, isolating per-type validation and pricing logic from aggregation.\n**Mass Change**: 53 -> 55 (+2, justified by improved clarity/separation of concerns)\n**Tests**: All 13 passing (3 todo unaffected)"}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_16-23-48_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/catalog.spec.ts\nImplementation file: 