omer`, `items`... simplifying to declared locals+params used: `policyBase, raw, firstInsuranceSurcharge, loyaltyDiscount, customer, isFirstQuote, items` = 7) → 7\n- Invocations: 3 (`computeItemsBasePremium`, `computeRawPolicyPremium`, `roundPremium`) → 2×3 = 6\n- Conditionals: 2 (two ternaries) → 4×2 = 8\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0 (all `const`)\n\n**Total Mass: 6 + 7 + 6 + 8 = 27**\n\n**Applying Simple Design Rules**:\n1. **Tests Pass**: All 7 tests green before starting. ✅\n2. **Reveals Intent**: The function body mixed three distinct concerns inline (base premium computation, first-insurance surcharge conditional, loyalty discount conditional) — the two ternaries buried the conceptual structure of \"premium = raw + surcharge - discount, then fee.\" Extracting each conditional into a well-named helper (`computeFirstInsuranceSurcharge`, `computeLoyaltyDiscount`) makes each rule independently readable and testable, and makes `computeQuotePremium` itself read as a straight-line composition.\n3. **No Duplication**: No duplicated logic found; each surcharge/discount rule is expressed once.\n4. **Fewest Elements**: Two new small helpers are justified — they encapsulate two independently named business rules (first-insurance surcharge, loyalty discount) referenced in the spec, improving readability without adding real complexity.\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Extracted `computeFirstInsuranceSurcharge(policyBase, isFirstQuote)` and `computeLoyaltyDiscount(customer, policyBase)` helper functions.\n- `computeQuotePremium` body is now a clear, flat sequence of named steps with no inline conditionals.\n\n**Refactored Code Mass** (`computeQuotePremium` body only, after refactor):\n```\nconst policyBase = computeItemsBasePremium(items);\nconst raw = computeRawPolicyPremium(items);\nconst firstInsuranceSurcharge = computeFirstInsuranceSurcharge(policyBase, isFirstQuote);\nconst loyaltyDiscount = computeLoyaltyDiscount(customer, policyBase);\nreturn roundPremium(raw + firstIns